# 9. Explainable Artificial Intelligence

Implement SHAP-based explainability.

The system should explain why an order received its risk score.

Example:

"Transaction flagged because of unusually high order value, multiple failed payment attempts and repeated suspicious activity from the same device."

Requirements:

- Feature importance.
- Individual prediction explanations.
- Risk factor visualization.
- Positive and negative feature contributions.
- Model version information.
- Explanation history.

The AI explanation must be generated from actual model and rule outputs, not invented text.

# 10. Machine Learning Dataset and Evaluation

Use public datasets and synthetic e-commerce transaction data.

Suggested datasets:

1. Credit Card Fraud Detection Dataset.
2. IEEE-CIS Fraud Detection Dataset.
3. Synthetic home decor e-commerce transactions.

Dataset sources:

- https://www.kaggle.com/datasets/mlg-ulb/creditcardfraud
- https://www.kaggle.com/competitions/ieee-fraud-detection

The system must implement:

- Data preprocessing.
- Missing-value handling.
- Feature engineering.
- Class imbalance handling.
- Train/validation/test splitting.
- Model training.
- Model comparison.
- Model persistence.
- Model versioning.

Evaluation metrics:

- Accuracy.
- Precision.
- Recall.
- F1-score.
- PR-AUC.
- ROC-AUC.
- Confusion matrix.
- False positive rate.
- False negative rate.

Precision and recall must receive particular attention because fraud datasets are usually highly imbalanced.

Model evaluation must use leakage-resistant splitting, preferably time-based when the dataset supports it.

